
# Kconfig file for FAL (Flash Abstraction Layer) package
menuconfig RT_USING_FAL
    bool "FAL: Flash Abstraction Layer"
    default n
    help
        FAL (Flash Abstraction Layer) is a flash device management framework that provides
        a unified interface for accessing different types of flash memory devices.

        Features:
        - Unified flash device access interface
        - Partition management and table support
        - Support for multiple flash device types (SPI NOR, NAND, etc.)
        - Portable across different hardware platforms
        - Integration with RT-Thread file systems

        This option enables the FAL framework for flash memory management in RT-Thread.

if RT_USING_FAL
    config FAL_USING_DEBUG
        bool "Enable debug log output"
        default y if RT_USING_DEBUG
        default n
        help
            Enable debug logging functionality for the FAL (Flash Abstraction Layer).

            When enabled, FAL will output detailed debug information including:
            - Flash device operations status
            - Partition creation and management operations
            - Error conditions and warnings
            - Device initialization information

            This option is automatically enabled when RT_USING_DEBUG is set to y.
            Debug output helps developers diagnose flash-related issues during development.

    config FAL_PART_HAS_TABLE_CFG
        bool "Use partition table defined in fal_cfg.h"
        default y
        help
            Select this option when the flash partition table is manually defined
            in the 'fal_cfg.h' configuration file.

            When enabled (y): The partition table information is read from the static
            definitions in fal_cfg.h. This is suitable for systems with fixed partition
            layouts that are known at compile time.

            When disabled (n): FAL will automatically search for and load a partition
            table from a specified location in flash memory. This is useful for dynamic
            partition configurations or when the partition table needs to be stored
            persistently in flash.

            This option affects how the system initializes and discovers flash partitions
            during startup.

    if !FAL_PART_HAS_TABLE_CFG

        config FAL_PART_TABLE_FLASH_DEV_NAME
            string "Flash device name for partition table storage"
            default "onchip"
            help
                Specify the name of the flash device where the partition table is stored.

                FAL will search for the partition table on this specific flash device.
                The device name must match one of the flash devices registered with FAL.

                Examples:
                - "onchip" for internal on-chip flash
                - "norflash0" for external SPI NOR flash
                - "nandflash" for NAND flash devices

                This option is only relevant when FAL_PART_HAS_TABLE_CFG is disabled,
                allowing dynamic partition table discovery from flash.

        config FAL_PART_TABLE_END_OFFSET
            int "Partition table search end offset"
            default 65536
            help
                Specify the end offset address (relative to the flash device start) from which
                FAL will begin searching backward for the partition table.

                FAL searches for the partition table by scanning backward from this offset
                address toward the beginning of the flash device. The search continues until
                a valid partition table is found or the beginning of flash is reached.

                Default: 65536 (64KB) - Suitable for most applications where the partition
                table is stored near the end of the first 64KB of flash.

                Considerations:
                - Ensure this offset doesn't overlap with critical system data or code
                - Larger values may increase search time on devices with large flash
                - The partition table must be located before this offset address

                This option is only used when FAL_PART_HAS_TABLE_CFG is disabled.

    endif

    config FAL_USING_SFUD_PORT
        bool "Enable SFUD (Serial Flash Universal Driver) support"
        default n
        help
            Enable SFUD driver integration for accessing SPI NOR flash devices.

            SFUD (Serial Flash Universal Driver) is a universal SPI NOR flash driver
            that supports a wide range of SPI NOR flash chips from various manufacturers.

            When enabled, FAL will use the SFUD porting implementation (fal_flash_sfud_port.c)
            from the samples/porting directory to interface with SFUD-compatible flash devices.

            Benefits:
            - Automatic flash chip detection and initialization
            - Support for many SPI NOR flash manufacturers (Winbond, GigaDevice, MXIC, etc.)
            - Unified interface for different flash operations
            - Simplified flash device management

            Use this option when your system uses SPI NOR flash devices that are
            compatible with the SFUD driver framework.

    if FAL_USING_SFUD_PORT
        config FAL_USING_NOR_FLASH_DEV_NAME
            string "SFUD NOR flash device name for FAL"
            default "norflash0"
            help
                Specify the name of the SPI NOR flash device that FAL should use
                when SFUD support is enabled.

                This name must match the device name registered by the SFUD driver
                in your RT-Thread system. The device name is typically set during
                SFUD initialization.

                Common examples:
                - "norflash0" - Default for first SPI NOR flash
                - "norflash1" - For second SPI NOR flash (if present)
                - "spiflash" - Custom device name

                This option is only available when FAL_USING_SFUD_PORT is enabled.
                Make sure the specified device name matches your actual hardware
                configuration and SFUD driver setup.
    endif

endif

